  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday Night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1152 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nHigh pressure will start to break down on Thursday ahead of a \npattern change that will push a front through the area. Before this \nfront however, Thursday will still be quite hot. Highs will remain \nin the low to mid 90s with heat index values slightly lower, but \nstill in the triple digit range. A shortwave will dip into the area \nThursday afternoon, bringing a more widespread chance for rain and \nstorms back into the forecast. Rain chances will jump up to the 60-\n80% range Thursday evening into Friday out ahead of a weak cold \nfront that will eventually push through Friday. The threat for \nsevere weather with this system continues to look low, though there \nwill be a bump in bulk shear values to around 30 kts. Mid-level \nlapse rates are poor, generally less than 6 C/Km, but ample \ninstability and lift will be in place to help get some storms going. \nThe strongest storms could produce gusty winds. Low to medium rain \nchances will continue through the day Friday as the front pushes \nsoutheast with chances areawide between 30-50%. Moving into the \nweekend, weak shortwaves will continue to dip into the area, keeping \n20-40% rain and storm chances in the forecast through Sunday. High \npressure will also start building east back into the area, sending \nour temperatures gradually back up into the mid 90s by Sunday.",
